--[[
`io.http` sends HTTP requests through the selected host implementation
without blocking the caller's frame. Reach for it when a program needs a client
that works the same in a CLI, a scheduler and a game host.

```nupp
local http = io.http
local uri = io

local client = http.newclient()
local response = assert(client:send(new http.Request(
    url = assert(uri.newuri("https://example.com/"))
)))
print(response.status)
response:close()
client:close()
```

Calls suspend through
[`coroutine.suspend`](docs/learn/runtime/concurrency/suspension.md): a CLI blocks
on the provider's condvar, while a scheduler or frame host only drives the
nonblocking source.

A response status is an answer, 4xx and 5xx included. Transport and body
failures are returned as reasons instead. Response bodies and generic request
readers are progressive and bounded, so a large body is read in pieces against
a limit rather than assembled first.

## Provider capabilities

The selected provider shares the request options and body declarations on this
page. `http.capabilities()` reports whether it supports streaming requests,
streaming responses, transport policy, connection policy, and protocol-version
reporting. Check those guarantees before depending on a host-specific facility.

The native provider supports all five. Browser Fetch accepts byte-string uploads
and buffers the response before returning it. The browser controls connections,
redirects, proxies, certificate policy, and decompression; options that try to
configure those policies are rejected. Browser responses report `version = nil`
because Fetch does not expose the negotiated protocol version.

## Requests that go out together

Requests share a client and go out together when something drives them
together. A [task scope](docs/learn/runtime/concurrency/task-scopes.md) runs each body in a
coroutine of its own, and the one waiting on the network is what lets the next
one send:

```nupp
local http = io.http
local tasks = coroutine
local uri = io

local function storeStatus(client: alias(http.Client), codes: {integer}, index: integer, url: string): nil
    local status = client:with(function(borrows open: http.Client): integer
        local response = assert(open:send(new http.Request(url = assert(uri.newuri(url)))))
        local code = response.status
        response:close()

        return code
    end)
    codes[index] = assert(status)
end

local client = own.manage(http.newclient())
local codes: {integer} = {}
do
    local scope = tasks.scope()
    scope:spawn(client:alias(), codes, 1, "https://example.com/", storeStatus)
    scope:spawn(client:alias(), codes, 2, "https://example.org/", storeStatus)
end

print(codes[1], codes[2])
```

A child cannot be handed an owner or a borrow, so the client goes into a managed
cell and each child gets a copyable alias to it. `with` lends the client for one
callback, and the cell closes the client when `client` leaves its scope.

Two bounds decide how much of that happens at once. `maxconnectionsperhost`
bounds what one host is asked to carry, and a scope's `limit` bounds how many
requests are in flight at all.

## Ownership

A client, request, response, streaming request body, and response body are
owners, so each closes at its lexical boundary unless it is transferred. A file
body is an immutable path value with no cleanup obligation. Closing a request
also closes a reader body transferred into it.

::: seealso
- [ownership.md](docs/learn/runtime/ownership/borrowing.md) for the complete contract
  reference
- [suspension.md](docs/learn/runtime/concurrency/suspension.md) for what a wait
  does under a handler
- [](io) for the URI values a request is addressed with
:::
]]
module io.http

local source1 = require("nupp.io.http")
export type Body = source1.Body
export type Capabilities = source1.Capabilities
export type Client = source1.Client
export type FileBody = source1.FileBody
export type Options = source1.Options
export type Reader = source1.Reader
export type ReaderBody = source1.ReaderBody
export type Request = source1.Request
export type RequestBody = source1.RequestBody
export type Response = source1.Response
export type Version = source1.Version
export const Capabilities = source1.Capabilities
export const FileBody = source1.FileBody
export const ReaderBody = source1.ReaderBody
export const Request = source1.Request
export const capabilities = source1.capabilities
export const file = source1.file
export const newclient = source1.newClient
export const reader = source1.reader
